course: adult-life
lesson: 6
title: Paying for a home and the bills
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Living in a home costs money every single month, even when nobody buys anything new. There is rent or a mortgage, energy for heating and hot water, council tax, broadband and insurance.
explain: Some families rent their home from a landlord; others are buying it with a huge loan from a bank called a mortgage. Both are normal ways to live.
explain: Most bills leave the bank account by themselves, by direct debit, so the money seems to vanish without anyone paying at a till.
explain: Example: in a terraced house in Leeds, Mum opens the energy bill at the kitchen table, goes quiet, then says "The electric's nearly doubled." That evening the heating goes down a degree, Dad watches the smart meter jump when the kettle boils, and everyone is told to keep the doors shut.
explain: None of this means the family is in trouble. It means grown-ups are steering the money so it lasts the whole month.
fact: Rent is paid every month to a landlord to live in a home you do not own, and the landlord may raise it within rules.
fact: Rent is often a family's biggest bill, so when it goes up, other spending has to shrink.
fact: A mortgage is a huge loan from a bank to buy a home, paid back monthly over about 25 years with interest; if payments stop for a long time the bank can take the home back.
fact: A landlord must keep a rented home safe and fix important things like the heating and leaks, though tenants sometimes have to chase repairs.
fact: Council tax is paid by each household to the local council and pays for bins, street lights, libraries, parks and care for older people.
fact: Energy bills pay for gas and electricity, and their prices rise and fall with world events.
fact: Heating a room with the window open sends the warm air, and the money that paid for it, straight outside.
fact: A direct debit lets a company take a bill from the bank account automatically, but there must be enough money in it on the day.
fact: A smart meter shows how much energy the house is using right now and what it costs, so families can see which things use the most.
fact: Broadband and phone deals last a year or two, and the price often jumps when the deal ends, so grown-ups shop around and switch.
fact: Insurance means many people pay a little regularly so the unlucky few get help after a fire, a theft or a crash; car insurance is required by law.
fact: A deposit for buying a home is often tens of thousands of pounds, and saving for it can take years.
fact: Hot water costs energy to heat, so long showers cost money and leave less for the next person.
exercise: Mum says "The rent's gone up again." What does it mean? | The family must pay the landlord more each month | The family now owns more of the house | The house has become bigger | The council has raised the bin charge
exercise: Why does a rent rise worry grown-ups so much? | Rent is often the biggest bill, so other spending must shrink | Rent rises mean the family must move out at once | Rent is paid only once, so it is a shock | Rent rises are paid by children's pocket money
exercise: Dad says "We've still got twenty years left on the mortgage." What is a mortgage? | A big bank loan for buying a home, paid back monthly | A monthly payment to a landlord for renting a home | A tax paid to the council for living in a house | A savings account that pays for house repairs
exercise: Why do grown-ups treat the mortgage so seriously? | If payments stop for long, the bank can take the home back | If they pay it off early, the bank sends them to court | The mortgage doubles every year if ignored | The council checks it every week
exercise: "The landlord still hasn't fixed the boiler." Whose job is it to fix it? | The landlord's, because they must keep the home safe | The family's, because they live there | The council's, because it pays for boilers | Nobody's, because boilers fix themselves
exercise: Mum says "The council tax is due." What does council tax pay for? | Bins, street lights, libraries and parks | The landlord's holidays abroad every summer | The family's own electricity and gas bills | The bank's interest on the family mortgage
exercise: Mum stares at a bill: "The electric's nearly doubled." What is she worried about? | The price of electricity has risen a lot | The house now has twice as many plugs as last year | The electricity will be cut off later tonight | The bill was sent to the wrong house by mistake
exercise: Dad shouts "Who left the heating on with the window open?" Why is he cross? | The warm air and the money are going outside | Open windows make the heating break down for good | The heating only works with windows shut by law | Cold air from outside makes the radiators explode
exercise: Mum says "It goes out by direct debit." What happens to the bill? | It is taken from the bank account automatically | It is paid in cash at the post office counter by Mum | It is cancelled and never paid | It is paid by the landlord instead
exercise: What must be true on the day a direct debit is taken? | There must be enough money in the account | Someone must sign a paper at the bank | The bill must be printed and posted | Mum must be at home to press a button
exercise: Dad watches the smart meter: "Look, the kettle makes it jump." What does the smart meter show? | How much energy the house is using right now | How much water is left in the kettle after you boil it | How many people are at home | How hot the weather is outside
exercise: "We're switching broadband provider." Why do grown-ups switch? | The price often jumps when the old deal ends | The old internet stops working after exactly one year | Switching is required by law every year | The new one gives free televisions forever
exercise: "The insurance renewal has come through." What is insurance? | Paying a little regularly so that help comes after a disaster | A loan from the bank that must be paid back | A fine for not looking after your home properly | A gift of money from the council each year to every family in the town
exercise: Mum says "We're saving up for a deposit." What is a deposit for buying a home? | A large sum paid upfront, often tens of thousands of pounds | A small fee paid to the estate agent every single week | The last payment of the mortgage | A refund the bank gives when you move in
exercise: Your brother shouts "Who's used all the hot water?" Why does it matter? | Hot water costs energy, and the next person has none | Hot water runs out for the whole street at the same time | Cold water is dangerous to wash in | The landlord counts every shower
exercise: Which habit genuinely helps with the energy bill? | Closing windows and doors when the heating is on | Leaving lights on all day and night so the bulbs last longer | Boiling a full kettle for one cup | Opening windows to let the heating work harder
test: You overhear Mum on the phone: "Third time I've rung, the heating's still broken and it's freezing." Who is she probably ringing? | The landlord, who must fix the heating | The council, who own every boiler in every house | The bank, who pay for repairs | The school, who send an engineer
test: Your sister says "We pay council tax so the council fixes our telly." Put her right. | No, it pays for things like bins, street lights and libraries | Yes, the council fixes all tellies for free, as long as you ring | No, council tax only pays for the police | Yes, but only if the telly is new
test: Dad mutters "bloody energy prices, it's the war again". Why would a war far away change the bill? | Energy prices rise and fall with world events | Wars use up all the electricity in our street | The council adds a special war tax to every bill | Soldiers read the smart meters
test: Uncle says "I never switch, I'm loyal, they look after loyal customers." What is usually true? | Loyal customers often pay the most once the deal ends | Loyal customers always get the lowest price there is in the shop | Switching makes the internet slower forever | Companies give loyal customers free years
test: Nan says "Insurance is a waste, I've never claimed once in 40 years." What is the best reply? | It protects against a disaster that might have happened | She will get all the money back with interest | Insurance pays out every year to everyone | She should claim anyway to get her money back
test: Mum: "rent's gone up 100 quid, so no takeaway friday for a while." Why no takeaway? | More money goes on rent, so extras must shrink | Takeaways are banned by the council when the rent rises | The landlord does not allow takeaways | Takeaways cost more when rent rises
test: Dad sighs: "Mortgage, energy, council tax, all gone out the first of the month." Why did he not pay at a till? | They go out by direct debit automatically | He forgot to pay them at all and will get a letter | The bank paid them as a present | They are paid only once a year
test: Your cousin says "Renting means your family is poor and failed." Put him right. | Renting is a normal way to live for lots of families | Only families with no jobs at all rent the homes they live in | Renters never have to pay any bills | Renting is illegal in most countries
test: A friend says "Close the window? Nah, just turn the heating up, it's free." What is wrong? | Heating costs money and the warmth escapes outside | Heating is only free in winter, when it is cold outside | Open windows make heating cheaper | Nothing is wrong, the landlord pays for it
test: Mum: "No holiday abroad this year, we're putting it all towards the house deposit." What is going on? | They are saving for a big sum to buy a home | The house deposit is a holiday company that sells trips | They have lost all their money for good | The landlord has asked for a holiday fee
test: You leave the kettle half full for your one cup of tea, and Dad says "good lad". Why? | Boiling only the water you need uses less energy | A full kettle can break the smart meter and the fuse box | Half-full kettles boil hotter water | The landlord only allows half a kettle
exam: Identify the principal consequence where a household ceases to remit mortgage repayments over a prolonged period. | The lending bank may repossess the dwelling | The council assumes ownership of the dwelling | The debt is simply cancelled after six months | The landlord becomes liable for the whole loan
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises council tax? | A charge levied on each household to fund local services | A payment to a landlord for the occupation of a rented dwelling | A charge for the consumption of gas and electricity | A premium remitted to an insurer annually
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning payment by direct debit. | Sums are withdrawn automatically, provided sufficient funds exist | Payment requires the attendance of the account holder in person at the bank | The bill is settled by the service provider itself | Payment is made solely in cash at a post office
exam: Ascertain the purpose of an insurance policy in respect of a household. | To share risk, so that losses from misfortune are largely covered | To accumulate savings towards a future house deposit for the household | To discharge the monthly rent owed to a landlord | To reduce the quantity of energy consumed
